  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA warm afternoon is ongoing, with temperatures ranging from the \nupper 70s to mid 80s. Responsible for this warmth is an upper level \nridge, but it will not last long. A deepening trough and area of low \npressure to our northwest will drive a strong cold front through the \nNorthern Plains and Upper Midwest Friday. High temperatures will \nvary considerably across the FA, ranging from the 60s in the Devils \nLake Basin, to near 90 in the southern Red River Valley, where the \nfront will not propagate through until mid or even late afternoon. \nAlong the front, forcing should be sufficient for thunderstorm \ndevelopment, some of which may become strong to severe. Thereafter, \ntemperatures will remain seasonably cool into the weekend. Moisture \nwill advect northward late Sunday into Monday ahead of a strong \nupper level low to our west that will bring widespread rainfall to \nend the weekend and start the work week. Temperatures will remain \nseasonal to just a few degrees cooler then seasonal into next week. \n\n...Near Critical Fire Weather this Afternoon...\n\nFor areas experiencing the most severe impacts from drought in \nsoutheastern ND, fuels have dried considerably. RH values this \nafternoon have dropped as low as 25 percent. Combine these \nfactors with a steady 10 to 20 mph southerly breeze, near \ncritical fire weather conditions are ongoing, and will continue \nthrough sunset. \n\n...Severe Storms Friday...\n\nAhead of a strong cold front, an axis of instability (2000+ J/Kg of \nMUCAPE) will be in place over far southeastern ND into much of\nwest central and northwestern MN. Overhead, increasing midlevel\nwest to southwest winds will improve shear profiles, promoting \n40 knots or so of effective shear. Shear profiles will likely \nvary across the FA. Hodographs show this well, with southern \nareas containing low level curvature, while northern areas \n(approximately US Highway 2 north) feature straighter \nhodographs. This will play into hazard type, with SPC \nprobabilities highlighting a 5% tornado risk extending from \nsouthern Beltrami County southward throughout the Lakes Country,\nwithin the area of better low level shear. With shear \norientation off the initiating front, supercells at least \ninitially are expected, but the strength of forcing and \nsplitting storms in the north may result in a messier cluster \nmode within a few hours of convective initiation.\n\nWhile the environment in west central MN certainly has a higher \nceiling due to the shear profiles and richer instability, there are \nquestions on if storms will form. This concern is two fold. First, \nif the front pushes through a bit faster (some latest CAMs are \nshowing this, especially within the REFS), storms may not form \nin our area, but rather our eastern border into DLH/MPX's areas.\nSecond, soundings indicate that capping will be present, with \ncapping becoming stronger the further south you go within the \nFA. Therefore, if a combination of a faster front/stronger \ncapping comes into play, the severe risk will become more \nlimited in west central MN, leaving more of a hail/wind risk in \nthe north. \n\n...Soaking Rainfall Early Next Week...\n\nA large upper low over the Pacific Northwest will propagate eastward \ntowards the Northern Plains late Sunday into Monday. There is\nsome uncertainty in where the deformation band will set up, with\nNBM probabilities in the 50 to 70 percent range across the\nentire FA. Instability while present, will not be sufficient for\nany severe weather concerns.",
